racy theories  emerging since the 1970s  and you find their endpoint in trump  i want to get back to the education  piece because  i have so many notes about it one small  thing that i was thinking of in your  your cultural idea of the kind of  individualization of problems is this  related to the gun issue in the united  states in this specific sense that  unlike in many other countries  too many people in the u.s see guns as a  way to solve problems that are that are  really systemic problems but they say i  will solve individually this problem  with a firearm  i think it's a brilliant analysis and i  think it's right on target i i think  that basically when you individualize  problems you say that people have to  take are both responsible for those  problems individually and have the right  to engage them in ways that suggest that  the government is the enemy of freedom  rather than something that could be  useful in dealing with these issues the  the other side of this is that  when you individualize problems in this  way  you can't address them systemically so  you begin to increasingly criminalize  social problems  so you know if youth for instance acting  out in schools you know the principal  doesn't come down and say hey come on  let's talk you call the police and all  of a sudden you have little  five-year-olds in handcuffs in  kindergarten uh so it seems to me with  this increasing criminalization of  social problems you have a notion of  freedom emerging that is completely  removed from this any notion of social  responsibility right a notion of freedom  that simply says i can do what i want  self-interest is all that matters uh all  problems are individualized this is what  i call the individualization of the  social and in the social sphere and the  social contract and the social good  and the public good david begin to  collapse  we lose those sites where people can  understand the interconnections that  make a democracy possible couple that  with an 