ople the opportunity to be  able to recognize that the most  fundamental questions are not whether  they're falling for lies but what kind  of future do they want for their  children do they want health care that's  free do they want to be in debt  for the rest of their lives critical  consciousness matters but it has to  translate ideas have to translate and be  wedded to power and it seems to be when  we remove the question of power from the  question of insight we fall into a trap  that we unconsciously reproduce that we  may want to stay away from and that is  to say well just talk to each other no  i'm sorry it's not about just talking  about each other it's about changing the  very institutions that have put us there  in the first place and that's not just  the burden of people who are confused  that's that's a political obligation  that we all have to engage in with those  people hopefully so in that sense i mean  when when you hear these very  superficial narratives of part of the  problem is  left and right don't engage anymore or  the the dialogue has coarsened this type  of stuff  is does that not really strike you as  particularly important that's a  diversion i find that as a political  diversion because it doesn't deal with  fundamental questions like look what's  the result of massive inequality in the  united states and where does it come  from right what is it what does it mean  when some people are trading medicine  for for  you know for food stamps you know what  does it mean when schools are collapsing  you know that's not a matter of whether  the right and left can talk to each  other that's a matter of how do we talk  about democracy as a country if we're  going to take democracy seriously and  what are the forces that are undermining  it these are very different kinds of  questions what does it mean to live in a  country where economic activity is  removed from social cost what's the  consequence of that and how does it bear  down on our lives