imply because  fashion what i call fascist politics  emerges but doesn't present itself in an  exact replica does not mean it doesn't  exist it means that it's always in  remission but tends to emerge in  different forms  and i think that what we have to look at  are the signals we have to look at the  similarities and certainly he's  absolutely right as is timothy schneider  jason stanley and a number of others who  were saying hey look look at this  language lies how central with the  question of lies in for instance in the  nazi regime racial purity racial  cleansing what does that tell us where  did that go in the 1930s and 40s  the appeal to militarism hyper  masculinity the attack on women's bodies  the attack on others the friend enemy  distinction  uh the the ongoing attempt to demonize  people and to claim that the public's  fear is only for white people my god i  mean if that doesn't conjure up an image  of the past and echoes of the past that  we should take seriously then they don't  if you don't believe in that analysis  you're going to have to end up in a cell  somewhere before you actually realize  right that fascist politics is here  alive well and throbbing think about the  january 6th  attack  i mean even robert o paxton who first  was the great historian of fascism who  said no trump is not a fascist has come  out recently and said i was wrong  i'm sorry the line has been crossed we  are basically now in a fascist culture  and fascist politics is on the rise of  the united states and represents a very  dangerous moment  uh the latest book from henry giroux is  race politics and pandemic pedagogy  published by bloomsbury we've been  speaking with henry drew chaired  professor for scholarship in the public  interest at mcmaster university it's  been so great having you on i can't tell  you how much i appreciate your time  today  oh thank you david it's a pleasure
 

